WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.440
Here is a loop. When NF-κB switches on, one of its target genes encodes tumor

2
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.520
necrosis factor, or TNF.

3
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.160
TNF is a cytokine, which is a signaling protein that immune cells secrete.

4
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.040
Where does it go next?

5
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:26.783
TNF binds TNF receptor 1 on the same cell, and on its neighbors.

6
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:34.043
That receptor also signals to IKK and NF-κB. So the alarm spreads.

7
00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:37.383
But this receptor carries a second message.

8
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:41.457
TNF receptor 1 is a death receptor.

9
00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:44.257
It can instruct a cell to kill itself.

10
00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:46.477
Usually it does not.

11
00:00:47.337 --> 00:00:51.877
NF-κB switches on genes whose products block the death machinery.

12
00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:56.837
So the receptor says die and stay alive at once.

13
00:00:57.617 --> 00:00:59.357
The second message normally wins.

14
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:09.120
Now take cultured cells whose NF-κB arm is blocked, for example by an IκB that cannot be phosphorylated.

16
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.960
The same TNF kills.

17
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.340
The death arm belongs to a later lesson.

18
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.540
Keep this rule. NF-κB normally makes the stay-alive message win.
